<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img src="/assets/img/photos/interior.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/interior-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/interior.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="800" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.1"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/resources/">Resources</a></li><li aria-current="page">FAQ</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker kicker-l">Answers</p><h1>Colorado Insurance Appraisal FAQ</h1><p class="lede">20 plain answers to the questions we hear most, grouped by topic: the process, costs and timing, perils, and Colorado-specific rules.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line-l" href="/contact-us/">Free claim review</a></div></div><svg class="ridge" viewBox="0 0 1440 90" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M0 90V62l110-30 60 22 130-50 90 44 60-16 120 38 150-62 80 34 90-20 130 44 110-34 70 18 140-48 100 40v48z"/></svg></section>
<div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose"><nav class="toc" aria-label="FAQ topics"><p class="toc-t">Topics</p><ul><li><a href="#faq-1">How appraisal works</a></li><li><a href="#faq-2">Costs and timing</a></li><li><a href="#faq-3">Hail, wildfire and other perils</a></li><li><a href="#faq-4">Colorado specifics</a></li></ul></nav><h2 id="faq-1">How appraisal works</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>What is insurance appraisal?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>A process in most property policies for resolving disagreements about the amount of a covered loss. Each side names an appraiser, the appraisers choose an umpire, and an award signed by two of the three sets the amount.</p></div></details><details><summary>Who can demand appraisal?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Usually either party — you or the insurer — can demand it in writing once you disagree on the amount of loss. Check your policy for any conditions that must be met first.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is appraisal the same as arbitration?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Not exactly. Appraisal is narrower: it focuses on valuing the loss and is usually less formal than arbitration, with no hearings in the courtroom sense.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can I be my own appraiser?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Most policies require a competent and impartial appraiser, which effectively rules out the policyholder. Hiring an experienced independent appraiser is the norm.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does the insurer’s appraiser work for the insurer?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>The insurer selects and pays its appraiser, just as you select and pay yours. Both are expected to be impartial under most policy wording.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="faq-2">Costs and timing</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>How much does an appraiser cost?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Fee arrangements vary — flat fees, hourly rates or a percentage of the increase. Whatever the basis, get it in writing before work starts. Umpire fees are usually split.</p></div></details><details><summary>How long does appraisal take?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Many residential files resolve in two to three months after both appraisers are named. Large commercial, wildfire or contents-heavy claims can take longer.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is there a deadline to demand appraisal?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Some policies set one, and the policy’s suit-limitation clause still runs. Read your policy and calendar the dates with our timeline builder.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is the award paid right away?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Payment terms depend on the policy. The insurer pays the award less the deductible and prior payments; recoverable depreciation usually follows once repairs are done.</p></div></details><details><summary>When is a gap too small for appraisal?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>When fees would consume most of the increase. As a rough guide, gaps under about 10% or a few thousand dollars are often better handled with a documented supplement request.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="faq-3">Hail, wildfire and other perils</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Can appraisal decide whether my roof should be replaced?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Appraisal values the cost to repair or replace covered damage, so repair-versus-replace disputes are often part of it. Coverage questions, such as cosmetic exclusions, may not be.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does appraisal work for wildfire total losses?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Yes, for disputes about the cost to rebuild, debris removal or contents values within your limits. It can’t increase the limits themselves.</p></div></details><details><summary>What about smoke damage to a house that didn’t burn?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Smoke and ash cleaning or replacement disputes are a common appraisal topic, provided the insurer has accepted that a covered loss occurred.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can I use appraisal for a frozen-pipe claim?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>If coverage is accepted, yes. Freezing and vacancy exclusions are coverage questions that may need to be resolved first.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does appraisal apply to my car?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Some auto policies have appraisal clauses, but our work focuses on homes and commercial buildings.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="faq-4">Colorado specifics</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Who regulates insurers in Colorado?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>The Colorado Division of Insurance, part of the Department of Regulatory Agencies (DORA). It licenses insurers and adjusters and accepts consumer complaints.</p></div></details><details><summary>What is the Colorado FAIR Plan?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>A last-resort property insurance program created by the legislature in 2023 for owners who can’t find coverage in the regular market, particularly in high-wildfire-risk areas. It began offering policies in 2025.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does Colorado have a bad-faith law?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Yes. Colorado statutes address unreasonable delay or denial of covered benefits, alongside common-law bad-faith claims. Those are legal matters for an attorney, not an appraisal panel.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can my roofer pay my deductible?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>No. Colorado law prohibits roofing contractors from paying, waiving or rebating a homeowner’s insurance deductible.</p></div></details><details><summary>Who appoints an umpire if the appraisers can’t agree?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Under typical policy language, a court of record in the county where the property sits — in Colorado, usually the district court.</p></div></details></div></article>
